1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,173
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, you discovered the chouette, backgammon for a group.

2
00:00:07,873 --> 00:00:12,254
Today, we're facing an opponent who never sleeps: the computer.

3
00:00:13,849 --> 00:00:18,279
Playing against a bot isn't just something to do when nobody's around.

4
00:00:17,979 --> 00:00:21,347
Used well, it's the best teacher there is. Let's go!

5
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:28,802
The first reason to play against the computer: it's available at any time.

6
00:00:28,502 --> 00:00:32,399
No waiting, no schedule, the game starts whenever you want.

7
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:35,989
The second reason: there's no pressure at all.

8
00:00:35,689 --> 00:00:42,082
It's perfect for learning the rules, testing an opening or regaining confidence before facing real players.

9
00:00:43,899 --> 00:00:49,392
And the third reason: it's a demanding opponent. A bot never makes a careless mistake.

10
00:00:49,092 --> 00:00:53,680
Beating it means playing really well… with a little luck with the dice.

11
00:00:57,056 --> 00:01:01,605
On TickGammon, the bots are built on GNU Backgammon, often called gnubg.

12
00:01:01,305 --> 00:01:05,087
It's one of the strongest backgammon programs in the world.

13
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:11,356
It's free and open-source software, developed by a community of enthusiasts.

14
00:01:11,506 --> 00:01:18,041
Like the famous TD-Gammon before it, it uses neural networks, trained on a very large number of games.

15
00:01:18,693 --> 00:01:25,545
It evaluates each position, chooses the move that gives it the best chances, and also makes the doubling cube decisions.

16
00:01:25,245 --> 00:01:28,494
It's stronger than the vast majority of human players.

17
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:36,669
So, does the computer cheat with the dice? It's a question everyone asks one day.

18
00:01:37,865 --> 00:01:41,696
The answer is no. The bot has no advantage with the dice.

19
00:01:41,396 --> 00:01:44,794
It wins only through the quality of its decisions.

20
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:52,101
If it feels like it always gets the right roll, look instead at how it places its checkers.

21
00:01:51,801 --> 00:01:54,555
It puts them where lots of rolls work for it.

22
00:01:55,268 --> 00:02:02,135
Remember the lesson on probabilities? The bot counts everything, on every move. That's its real secret!

23
00:02:05,242 --> 00:02:09,722
The real value of the computer is that it shows you your mistakes.

24
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:13,016
After a game, you can have it analysed move by move.

25
00:02:14,463 --> 00:02:20,402
For each move, the analysis shows you the best possible choice, and compares it with the one you played.

26
00:02:20,918 --> 00:02:27,482
When you got it wrong, it tells you how much that mistake cost you. A small inaccuracy isn't a big deal.

27
00:02:27,182 --> 00:02:31,337
A big mistake, on the other hand, is one you need to understand.

28
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:37,078
Don't try to fix everything at once. Focus on the two or three biggest mistakes of the game.

29
00:02:36,778 --> 00:02:39,148
That's where you'll improve fastest.

30
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:46,064
An important tip: don't just look at the result of the game.

31
00:02:47,076 --> 00:02:50,852
A win can hide big mistakes, because the dice saved you.

32
00:02:50,552 --> 00:02:54,329
And a loss can come from the dice, despite perfect play.

33
00:02:56,164 --> 00:03:01,260
The result partly depends on luck. The analysis only looks at your decisions.

34
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:03,378
It's the one that tells the truth!

35
00:03:06,539 --> 00:03:09,981
On TickGammon, the Training module goes even further.

36
00:03:09,681 --> 00:03:13,894
It has you work on selected positions, and it analyses your moves.

37
00:03:15,283 --> 00:03:20,689
You play a position, then you see straight away whether your move was right, and what the best one was.

38
00:03:21,048 --> 00:03:26,543
It's the fastest way to fix your habits: openings, hitting, the race, the doubling cube…

39
00:03:26,243 --> 00:03:28,904
everything you've seen in these lessons.

40
00:03:30,464 --> 00:03:38,043
And with the Wisdom Pack, the artificial intelligence can even explain your mistake in plain language, and the analysis goes deeper.

41
00:03:39,393 --> 00:03:43,963
Instead of just a number, you understand why one move is better than another.

42
00:03:43,663 --> 00:03:46,237
And that's what really makes you improve.

43
00:03:48,702 --> 00:03:53,239
Here's a simple method for improving against the computer, in three steps.

44
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:59,722
Step one: play a full game, calmly, without looking at any hints during play.

45
00:04:00,419 --> 00:04:06,200
Step two: have the game analysed, and write down your two or three biggest mistakes.

46
00:04:07,262 --> 00:04:13,148
Step three: replay those positions in Training until you find the right move first time.

47
00:04:12,848 --> 00:04:15,243
Then start again with a new game.

48
00:04:18,847 --> 00:04:23,970
Are you a beginner, worried that it's too hard? It's normal to lose often at first.

49
00:04:25,167 --> 00:04:28,985
But every game against a strong opponent teaches you something.

50
00:04:28,685 --> 00:04:32,392
And little by little, your mistakes become rarer and smaller.

51
00:04:33,412 --> 00:04:41,021
If needed, go back over the previous lessons: the rules, the openings, the doubling cube, the race and the probabilities.

52
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:44,102
It all comes together on the board against the bot.

53
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:48,942
And when you feel ready, move on to online games against real players!

54
00:04:51,618 --> 00:05:01,493
And there you go, you now know how to use the computer to improve: an opponent that's always available, honest dice, and an analysis that shows you your mistakes.

55
00:05:01,643 --> 00:05:06,832
Games against the bots are free, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

56
00:05:06,532 --> 00:05:12,568
Next time, we move on to online games: rankings and ELO. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

57
00:05:12,268 --> 00:05:14,523
See you soon around the board!

